\section{Which pairs are joined by a line}{


\code{precrec} exists because the points of a precision-recall curve must not
be joined by straight lines. The metrics this function reads are raw
per-cutoff values with no interpolation, so joining an arbitrary pair
of them would be the very error the package was written to avoid.

Two pairs have a defined interpolation, and only those two are drawn as
curves: \code{x_metric = "fpr"} with \code{y_metric = "sensitivity"},
which is the ROC curve, and \code{x_metric = "sensitivity"} with
\code{y_metric = "precision"}, which is the precision-recall curve. For
those, \code{metric_curve} hands the work to the same code
\code{evalmod(mode = "rocprc")} uses, so the two cannot disagree.

Every other pair is drawn as points. Pass \code{type = "l"} to
\code{plot} or \code{autoplot} to join them anyway, having decided that
the straight lines mean something for the pair at hand.
}

\section{What this function does not do}{


\code{metric_curve} draws one curve per test dataset and does not average
over them. An average needs a rule for interpolating between the
points of each curve, which is exactly what an unregistered pair does
not have. Use \code{evalmod(calc_avg = TRUE)} for averaged ROC and
precision-recall curves.
}

